@baseUrl = http://localhost:5128
@HostAddress = {{baseUrl}}
@companyAdminEmail = admin@al-salamah.com
@companyAdminPassword = User@123456
@missingJob = 4c8b2d33-9e55-4f29-ae16-7d3f9b6a2c81
@missingUser = 7d3f9b6a-2c81-4f29-ae16-4c8b2d339e55

### =====================================================================
### 20. NOTIFICATIONS: THE INBOX AND THE DELIVERY LOG
### =====================================================================
### Two endpoints over one table, with deliberately different authorization.
###
### The INBOX (GET /notifications) carries no permission. It holds only what was addressed to
### the caller, identified from the token, so "any authenticated user" is the correct and
### complete answer — there is no wider set of rows they could reach. A permission here would
### mean a role could exist that receives notifications it cannot read.
###
### The OUTBOX (GET /notifications/outbox) is gated by Notifications.Audit. It holds messages
### addressed to other people, with their e-mail addresses and the message bodies, so it is a
### privilege over someone else's correspondence and is gated like the audit trail. Granted by
### default to CompanyAdmin and DeputyAdmin.
###
### Why the outbox exists at all: NotificationLog records every attempt, including the failures
### and the events that found no channel configured, so the platform can answer "was the client
### actually told?" months later. Until it shipped, nothing could ask — the inbox filters to
### InApp and to the caller, so every e-mail, SMS and WhatsApp attempt was written and then
### unreadable. It is also how a transport gets commissioned: a failed send names its own
### cause here (`SMTP 535` is a wrong password, `550` a rejected recipient), which beats going
### to look in a mailbox the tester does not own.
###
### Ordering and filtering are covered by NotificationOutboxTests (xUnit), including the tenant
### boundary. This suite is a smoke test of the surface with explicit assertions. Field casing
### is camelCase (D-8).

### 20.1 Company Admin login
# @name companyAdminLogin
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "email": "{{companyAdminEmail}}", "password": "{{companyAdminPassword}}" }

?? status == 200

@companyAdminToken = {{companyAdminLogin.data.token}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### Authentication
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### 20.2 Unauthenticated inbox -> 401
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications

?? status == 401

### 20.3 Unauthenticated mark-read -> 401
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/mark-read
Content-Type: application/json

{ "notificationIds": [] }

?? status == 401

### 20.4 Unauthenticated delivery log -> 401
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox

?? status == 401

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### The inbox: the caller's own, no permission needed
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### 20.5 Own inbox -> 200
### unreadCount counts every unread notification, not just the page, so the badge stays right
### when the list is filtered.
# @name inbox
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? body isSuccess == true
?? js response.parsedBody.data.unreadCount > -1
?? js response.parsedBody.data.items.length > -1

### 20.6 Unread only -> 200
### A subset of 20.5 by construction, so the count can never exceed it.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications?unreadOnly=true
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? js response.parsedBody.data.items.length <= {{inbox.data.items.length}}

### 20.7 pageSize is clamped, not refused -> 200
### GetMyNotificationsQuery normalises in the handler rather than validating (it is named in
### PagingValidationTests.ClampedInHandler), so an absurd page size is silently reduced to 200.
### The outbox behaves differently on purpose — see 20.12.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications?pageSize=100000
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? js response.parsedBody.data.items.length <= 200

### 20.8 Mark everything read -> 200
### An empty list means "mark all read". Returns how many were NEWLY marked.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/mark-read
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "notificationIds": [] }

?? status == 200
?? body isSuccess == true

### 20.9 Marking again is idempotent -> 200, and newly-marked is 0
### Already-read notifications are not re-stamped, so the first read is the one recorded and a
### client that retries gets zero rather than an error.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/mark-read
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "notificationIds": [] }

?? status == 200
?? body data == 0

### 20.10 An id belonging to someone else matches nothing -> 200, 0 marked
### Not 403 and not 404. The handler filters by the caller's own id regardless of the ids sent,
### so a foreign id is simply not found — rather than needing a separate authorization check
### that a future edit could forget.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/mark-read
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "notificationIds": ["{{missingUser}}"] }

?? status == 200
?? body data == 0

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### The delivery log: permission-gated, company-wide
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### 20.11 Delivery log as Company Admin -> 200
# @name outbox
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? body isSuccess == true
?? js response.parsedBody.data.items.length > -1
?? js response.parsedBody.data.totalCount > -1
?? js response.parsedBody.data.pageNumber > 0

### 20.12 Invalid paging is refused, not clamped -> 400
### The opposite of 20.7, and deliberate: this query validates rather than normalising, because
### it is new and has no client relying on the old behaviour. pageNumber=0 and pageSize=-5 once
### reached SQL Server as a negative OFFSET and a zero-row FETCH, and five list endpoints
### answered 500.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?pageNumber=0
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 400
?? body isSuccess == false

### 20.13 Negative page size -> 400
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?pageSize=-5
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 400

### 20.14 Page size above the ceiling -> 400
### PagingRules.MaxPageSize is 100, shared by every paged list.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?pageSize=101
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 400

### 20.15 The ceiling itself is accepted -> 200
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?pageSize=100
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200

### 20.16 Filter by delivery outcome -> 200, and every row matches
### `?status=Failed` is the common question. Enums are integers on the wire (Docs/APIs/ENUMS.md):
### Pending=1, Sent=2, Failed=3, NoChannelConfigured=4.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?status=3
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? js response.parsedBody.data.items.filter(n => n.status != 3).length < 1

### 20.17 Filter by transport -> 200, and every row matches
### Channel: InApp=1, Email=2, Sms=3, WhatsApp=4.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?channel=2
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? js response.parsedBody.data.items.filter(n => n.channel != 2).length < 1

### 20.18 Filter by job -> 200
### A job that does not exist is an empty page, not a 404: this is a filter over a log, not a
### lookup of a job.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?instanceId={{missingJob}}
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? body data.totalCount == 0

### 20.19 Filter by recipient -> 200
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?recipientUserId={{missingUser}}
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? body data.totalCount == 0

### 20.20 A non-numeric enum is a binding failure -> 400, in the envelope
### Binding failures used to leak internal type names; they are now in-envelope like every
### other error (D-8).
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?status=not-a-status
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 400
?? body isSuccess == false

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### A client has an inbox but not a delivery log
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### 20.21 Register a client for this suite
### Self-registered rather than assumed: seeded client accounts get Bogus-generated e-mail
### addresses, so only admin@ and deputy@ are deterministic. A suite that logs in as a fixed
### client account passes locally and fails in CI, and depending on another suite having run
### first is how one timeout takes four suites down with it.
# @name registerNotificationsClient
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/auth/register-client
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en
X-Tenant-Domain: al-salamah

{
  "fullName": "Notifications Suite Client",
  "email": "notifications.suite.client@example.com",
  "phoneNumber": "+966500200020",
  "password": "ClientSecure@123"
}

?? status == 200

@notificationsClientId = {{registerNotificationsClient.data.userId}}
@notificationsClientOtp = {{registerNotificationsClient.data.devOnlyOtp}}

### 20.21a Verify the OTP so the account can sign in
### devOnlyOtp above is populated by FeatureToggles:IsOtpReturnInsideTheResponse, which is on in
### Development and Staging and refused outside them by the startup guard. Without it this step
### is impossible and the whole suite stops here — which is exactly the state staging was in
### before 2026-09-30.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/auth/verify-client-otp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "userId": "{{notificationsClientId}}", "otp": "{{notificationsClientOtp}}" }

?? status == 200

### 20.22 Client login
# @name clientLogin
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en
X-Tenant-Domain: al-salamah

{ "email": "notifications.suite.client@example.com", "password": "ClientSecure@123" }

?? status == 200

@clientToken = {{clientLogin.data.token}}

### 20.23 A client reads their own inbox -> 200
### The inbox is not staff-only. A client is told when their quotation is ready, so they must be
### able to read it.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications
Authorization: Bearer {{clientToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 200
?? body isSuccess == true

### 20.24 A client against the delivery log -> 403
### The one assertion this suite exists for. A client who could read the outbox would read every
### message the company sent to every other client, including their names and e-mail addresses.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox
Authorization: Bearer {{clientToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 403

### 20.25 And filtering it to themselves is still 403
### The permission gates the endpoint, not the rows. There is no query string that turns a
### client into an auditor.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/notifications/outbox?recipientUserId={{missingUser}}
Authorization: Bearer {{clientToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 403
